WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.660
Welcome back to unintended consequences episode 4. We're looking at a case

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:09.720
today where the people involved were doing their best with the information they

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:16.560
had. In the winter of 1943 to 1944, US Army Typhus control teams and

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:22.560
Naples pumped handheld sprayers filled with a 10% DDT solution over the heads and

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:28.040
clothing of more than a million refugees and soldiers. Within weeks the

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:32.710
epidemic collapsed. The compound that had been synthesized only four years

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:38.490
earlier was hailed as a public health triumphant. Yet two decades later, field

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:43.110
biologists in the northeastern United States were cracking open eggs from

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:48.710
iris that contained no viable embryos. The shells were so thin they collapsed

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:53.730
under the weight of incubating parents. The substance designed to sever the

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:58.570
transmission of malaria and typhus had entered the fatty tissues of fish and

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:03.550
birds. Concentrating at each step up the food chain until it reached the top

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:11.110
predators whose reproductive failure no one had modeled. The Swiss chemist Paul

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:16.950
Herman Mieler, working at the J.R. Geigee laboratories in Basel, first

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:23.530
demonstrated DDT's insecticidal power in 1939 while screening compounds from

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:30.190
moth-proofing wool. At the time insect vectors still killed or disabled millions

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:36.810
each year. Laosborne typhus had devastated armies in the first world war and was

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:40.530
again threatening civilian populations displaced by the second.

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:46.670
Nieler's compound was inexpensive to manufacture. It appeared to have low acute

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:52.290
toxicity to mammals. It remained effective for weeks after a single application.

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:57.790
Public health authorities therefore sought as a rational weapon against an

00:01:57.790 --> 00:02:03.230
ancient enemy. When the US military adopted it for delousing and mosquito control

00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:07.770
in the Pacific and Mediterranean theaters the results were immediate and

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:13.550
measurable. Typhus cases in sprayed Italian cities fell from thousands to

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:20.910
near zero. In that context extending the same tool to agriculture after 1945 seemed

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:25.310
a logical next step for protecting food supplies for a world still recovering

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:33.710
from wartime shortages. Between 1945 and 1955 US production of DDT rose from

00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:39.310
roughly 10 million pounds to more than 100 million pounds annually. Much of it

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:45.670
was applied to cotton, potatoes and forests. The World Health Organization launched

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:52.850
its global malaria eradication program in 1955, relying on indoor residual

00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:59.050
spraying of DDT at 1 to 2 grams per square meter. Early results were dramatic.

00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:05.930
Sri Lanka reported only 17 malaria cases in 1963 after spraying began

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:13.350
down from 2.8 million in 1946. American newspapers and government films

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:19.670
carried slogans such as DDT is good for me while the US Department of Agriculture

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:26.110
conducted aerial campaigns against gypsy moths in the northeast and fire ants in the south.

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:33.970
A handful of entomologists noted the rapid appearance of resistant houseflies in Italy by 1947.

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:39.330
They warned that mosquitoes might follow. Yet these cautions were treated as

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:44.870
engineering problems solvable by higher doses or rotation of compounds.

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:51.610
At the time, no regulatory framework required testing for long-term ecological persistence

00:03:52.210 --> 00:04:00.770
or effects on non-target species. DDT is highly lipophilic and degrades slowly in the environment.

00:04:01.570 --> 00:04:08.270
Its metabolite DD interferes with calcium deposition during egg shell formation in birds of prey.

00:04:08.990 --> 00:04:14.910
By the early 1960s, egg shell thickness and paragrand falcons in Britain had declined by

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:22.530
approximately 20%. Nesting success in the eastern United States had fallen below replacement levels

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:32.270
in Clear Lake, California, a 1949 to 1957, NAT control program applied 40,000 pounds of DDT

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:38.130
a close relative. After that program, western grieve populations crashed.

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:44.470
Tissue samples showed concentrations 80,000 times higher than in the surrounding water.

00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:50.850
The same pattern appeared in estuaries and forests wherever repeated spraying occurred.

00:04:51.850 --> 00:04:57.890
Plankton absorbed trace amounts, fish concentrated them, fish eating birds received doses high

00:04:57.890 --> 00:05:03.590
enough to disrupt reproduction. Human breast milk samples collected in the United States and

00:05:03.590 --> 00:05:08.850
Western Europe during the 1960s routinely contained measurable DDT residues.

00:05:10.930 --> 00:05:15.510
The health consequences remained uncertain because the compound persisted for years.

00:05:16.170 --> 00:05:21.410
Each new application added to an accumulating burden rather than replacing an earlier one.

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:27.330
Resistance and target insects then prompted still heavier use accelerating the cycle.

00:05:27.890 --> 00:05:33.870
The second order effect was the loss of natural predators that had previously helped control

00:05:33.870 --> 00:05:40.810
agricultural pests. The third order effect was a broad public awakening to the possibility that synthetic

00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:46.430
chemicals could reorganize entire ecosystems in ways invisible at the point of application.

00:05:47.610 --> 00:05:55.050
Rachel Carson's Silent Spring serialized in the New Yorker in June 1962 and published as a book

00:05:55.050 --> 00:06:01.150
that September synthesized these field observations and forced the issue into national debate.

00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:06.370
President Kennedy directed the Science Advisory Committee to review the evidence.

00:06:06.790 --> 00:06:12.990
It's 1963 report confirmed the hazards to wildlife and called for tighter controls.

00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:21.850
The US Environmental Protection Agency newly created in 1970 held formal hearings and in 1972

00:06:21.850 --> 00:06:28.370
banned DDT for most agricultural uses while allowing continued public health applications.

00:06:29.190 --> 00:06:34.090
In some regions where spraying ceased abruptly, malaria incidents rose again.

00:06:35.110 --> 00:06:40.510
Sri Lanka recorded a resurgence to more than half a million cases by 1969.

00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:46.010
The meta-irony emerged decades later when the World Health Organization

00:06:46.570 --> 00:06:53.650
facing persistent malaria mortality in sub-Saharan Africa, endorsed limited indoor DDT spraying again

00:06:53.650 --> 00:06:58.830
in 2006 after newer insecticides proved less durable on mud walls.

00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:05.130
Today, DDT remains detectable in Arctic food webs and in human serum worldwide,

00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:09.810
even though its legal agricultural use has ended in most countries.

00:07:10.650 --> 00:07:15.730
The compound's environmental half-life has proven far longer than the institutions that

00:07:15.730 --> 00:07:23.390
first deployed it. Once a persistent, fat-soluble molecule is released into living systems,

00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:29.830
its movement through food webs and its cumulative dose cannot be predicted from short-term toxicity

00:07:29.830 --> 00:07:36.850
tests alone. Decision makers therefore need monitoring protocols that track second and third-order

00:07:36.850 --> 00:07:42.150
ecological changes rather than relying solely on the original target organism.

00:07:42.150 --> 00:07:47.830
At the same time, the urgency of controlling disease vectors remains real.

00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:54.270
The question is not whether to intervene but how to design interventions whose side effects can

00:07:54.270 --> 00:08:00.870
be detected and corrected before they scale. The DDT story continues to surface whenever new

00:08:00.870 --> 00:08:06.630
classes of pesticides, plastics, or pharmaceuticals are introduced at industrial volumes.

00:08:07.190 --> 00:08:13.910
The same tension between immediate measurable benefits and diffuse delayed consequences reappears.

00:08:14.570 --> 00:08:20.610
How might we build regulatory and scientific institutions that treat ecological persistence

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:25.150
as a design criterion rather than an after-the-fact surprise?

00:08:26.330 --> 00:08:31.650
That's unintended consequences for today. If this episode gave you something to think about,

00:08:32.210 --> 00:08:36.970
share it with a friend who'd appreciate the story. Subscribe wherever you listen and we'll

